data: {"content": "The classic ECG findings in hyperkalemia include peaked T waves, prolonged PR interval, widened QRS complex, loss of P waves, sine wave pattern, and eventually ventricular fibrillation or cardiac arrest. **Critical safety warning:** Hyperkalemia can be life-threatening; rapid treatment with measures such as calcium gluconate administration should be considered when EKG changes indicate severe hyperkalemia.\n\nDetails:"}

