data: {"structured_response": {"action_intent": "None.", "concise_answer": "The classic ECG findings in hyperkalemia include peaked T waves, prolonged PR interval, widened QRS complex, loss of P waves, sine wave pattern, and eventually ventricular fibrillation or cardiac arrest. **Critical safety warning:** Hyperkalemia can be life-threatening; rapid treatment with measures such as calcium gluconate administration should be considered when EKG changes indicate severe hyperkalemia.\n\nDetails:", "display_mode": "concise", "patient_facts_summary": "None", "local_policy_guidance": "None", "external_guidance": "None", "clinical_assessment": "The classical ECG signs of hyperkalemia are peaked T waves, prolonged PR interval, widening of the QRS complex, loss of P waves, and ultimately, sine wave patterns that may progress to ventricular arrhythmias like ventricular fibrillation/asystole. These changes occur due to potassium's effect on myocardial cell membrane potential and action potentials. It is important to note that these changes do not always appear together, and their presence depends on the severity of hyperkalemia. Treatment involves shifting potassium intracellularly (e.g., insulin + glucose) and removing it from the body (e.g., diuretics, dialysis). Calcium gluconate helps stabilize the myocardium against the effects of high potassium.", "caveats": "While classic findings exist, they aren't always present in all cases of hyperkalemia. Also, other conditions can mimic some of these ECG changes. Always consider the clinical context alongside the ECG.", "escalation": "If there are any concerns about a patient with suspected hyperkalemia, especially if accompanied by severe EKG abnormalities or symptoms such as muscle weakness or paralysis, immediate consultation with cardiology or critical care should be sought for further evaluation and management. A rapid assessment including serum potassium level is crucial.", "cited_sources": []}}

